Don't pass an 'I' modifier from a stat format to printf on Windows.
print_it() accepts the glibc 'I' flag (locale digits) between a '%'
and the directive letter and passes it on to printf. The Windows C
runtime instead takes 'I' as an integer size prefix (I, I32, I64), so
"stat -c %Is" built "%II64u" and printed "II64u" instead of the size.
On Windows, don't treat 'I' as a modifier: like any other letter that
is not a directive, it is then printed as itself.

---
 coreutils/stat.c |    3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/coreutils/stat.c
+++ b/coreutils/stat.c
@@ -463,7 +463,8 @@
 		}
 
 		/* dest = "%<modifiers>" */
-		len = 1 + strspn(p + 1, "#-+.I 0123456789");
+		/* (msvcrt printf takes 'I' as a size prefix, not a flag) */
+		len = 1 + strspn(p + 1, "#-+." IF_NOT_PLATFORM_MINGW32("I") " 0123456789");
 		memcpy(dest, p, len);
 		dest[len] = '\0';
 
